  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{y}+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x.$ Given that the point $(1,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, what is the value of ${{y}^{\u2033}|}_{(1,1)}?$",
  "worked_solution": "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{\u2032})^{\u2032} & ={(\\frac{1}{y})}^{\u2032}+{(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)}^{\u2032} \\\\ {y}^{\u2033} & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot {y}^{\u2032}+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the chain rule to compute ${(\\frac{1}{y})}^{\u2032}.$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{\u2032}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2033} & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot {y}^{\u2032}+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{\u2033} & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{y}+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{\u2033} & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{\u2033}|}_{(1,1)} & ={(-\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x})|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(1)^{3}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1)}{(1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(1)} \\\\  & =-1+0+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
